  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings and break-even analyses are based on a single geographic corridor between St. Louis and Louisiana, which may not represent corridors with different infrastructure densities or geography.",
      "what": "The Intermodal Freight Optimization and Routing Model (INFORM), a mixed-integer quadratic program, was developed to jointly minimize cost, transit time, and emissions while accounting for fuel technologies, facility siting, and up to two transfers per shipment. When applied to the St. Louis–Louisiana corridor, the model demonstrated that trucking remains the optimal choice under nominal conditions, and that rail and barge only become competitive at line-haul distances far exceeding those in the study corridor due to high access, egress, and transfer penalties.",
      "why": "The model demonstrates that the per-mile efficiency advantages of rail and barge are consistently negated by transfer frictions and access penalties, proving that intermodal infrastructure investments will fail to shift freight away from highways unless they directly target the structural cost and time advantages of trucking."
    }
  },
